##Readme

## 📊 FinQA LLM Evaluation

This project extends the FinQA benchmark by evaluating modern AI systems such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, and FinBERT on financial numerical reasoning tasks.

Instead of using the original program-based evaluation from FinQA, this project introduces a simplified evaluation pipeline that compares model outputs directly against ground-truth answers while also measuring response time.

## 🚀 Project Overview

The original FinQA paper evaluates models based on program generation and execution. However, modern large language models (LLMs) do not naturally produce structured programs.

This project introduces a new approach:

✅ Evaluate final answers instead of programs
✅ Compare multiple AI models
✅ Measure both accuracy and response time
✅ Analyze reasoning performance on financial data

## ⚙️ Setup Instructions

1. Install Python packages:
python -m pip install -r requirements_models.txt

2. Create a .env file:
Copy .env 

3. Add your API keys in .env:
OPENAI_API_KEY=...
GEMINI_API_KEY=...
GITHUB_TOKEN=...

4. Run live comparison:
python ask_live_compare.py --providers openai gemini github_models

5. Run dataset experiment:
python multi_model_compare.py --dataset dev.json --providers openai gemini github_models --limit 5 --out_dir outputs

6. Evaluate results:
python eval_all_models.py --dataset dev.json --predictions_dir outputs --out_dir eval_outputs

## 📄 Predictions Format

Your predictions file must be a JSON list like this:
[
{
"id": "ETR/2016/page_23.pdf-2",
"model": "chatgpt",
"predicted_answer": 94,
"response_time": 1.12
},
{
"id": "INTC/2015/page_41.pdf-4",
"model": "gemini",
"predicted_answer": "14%",
"response_time": 0.94
},
{
"id": "ABMD/2012/page_75.pdf-1",
"model": "copilot",
"predicted_answer": "yes",
"response_time": 1.48
}
]
Fields:
id → must match dataset ID
model → model name (chatgpt, gemini, copilot, etc.)
predicted_answer → model’s output
response_time → time taken to generate answer (seconds)

## 📐 Evaluation Method

Each prediction is compared with the ground truth:

Numeric answers → compared with tolerance
Yes/No answers → exact match
Percentages → normalized automatically

Accuracy is computed as:

Accuracy = Correct Predictions / Total Predictions

## 🧪 Methodology
Select a subset of FinQA questions
Generate prompts using dataset context
Collect responses from different AI models
Store outputs in preds.json
Evaluate using llm_eval.py

##🔥 Innovation

This project improves on the original FinQA research by:

Evaluating modern LLMs not available in the original paper
Removing dependency on program generation
Introducing response time analysis
Providing a unified benchmarking framework


